\pard\sb200\sa180\b\fs31 Backend SRS: Card Blocking and Legacy Client Compatibility\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Source: \\BACK_SRS_EXAMPLE_2.docx\\, STORAGE library v1.0.0 (September 2025). The original specifies \\POST card/block\\, legacy/new mobile-client behavior, a mandatory card identifier, card-reason values, card-network routing, card-state persistence and success/error mapping. This is a new bank-neutral educational edition: production endpoint paths, support contacts, exact internal database names, numeric status dictionaries and sample credentials are deliberately omitted.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 1. Purpose and business behavior\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Allow an authenticated customer to block a card for reasons such as temporary customer block, lost or stolen. The sample supports two client-contract generations with different input parameters.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 2. Compatibility model from the source\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Caller    |    Incoming information    |    Back-end behavior\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Legacy client    |    Card identifier and SMS confirmation, no explicit \\cardStatus\\    |    Verify an OTP/SMS challenge; infer the default customer-block reason\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 New client    |    Card identifier and explicit \\cardStatus\\ reason    |    Process the reason and apply the new client verification contract\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Invalid caller / card    |    Unauthenticated, unauthorized, unknown or ineligible card    |    Reject without changing card status\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Important: Skipping SMS in the newer branch is part of the original example's flow; it is not a recommendation to remove authentication or step-up verification. A production implementation must define permitted actions and security policy for each client version.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 3. Public conceptual request\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \\\\\\http\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 POST /v1/cards/\{cardId\}/block\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Authorization: Bearer <access_token>\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Content-Type: application/json\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Idempotency-Key: <uuid>\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \\\\\\\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \\\\\\json\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \{\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 "cardStatus": "LOST",\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 "reason": "Card cannot be located"\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \}\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 \\\\\\\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The endpoint, \\reason\\ and idempotency header are public illustrative additions, not an exact replica of the source interface.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 4. Processing steps\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 1. Validate caller and ownership of the card.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 2. Resolve client-contract version and required verification mechanism.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 3. Validate card identifier and eligible lifecycle transition.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 4. Map normalized block reason to provider/processor-specific status.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 5. Select the supported card-processing destination.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 6. Invoke processor card-status operation with protected correlation.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 7. Only after a confirmed positive result, update local card state and emit an auditable event.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 8. When a provider outcome is ambiguous, inquire/reconcile before assuming failure or reissuing the command.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The original names Visa/UnionPay route selection. Its precise processor-specific mapping values and card-table enumeration are excluded from this public contract.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 5. Business status mapping\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Incoming intent    |    Canonical public status\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Temporary customer block    |    \\BLOCKED_BY_CARDHOLDER\\\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Lost card    |    \\LOST\\\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Stolen card    |    \\STOLEN\\\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 A real processor may require entirely different numeric/textual reason codes; the adapter owns that translation.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 6. Error and recovery scenarios\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Failure    |    Required behavior\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Card cannot be found    |    No status update; controlled customer-facing not-found error\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 User lacks card ownership    |    Reject before external call\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Verification invalid or expired    |    Request re-verification\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Processor returns explicit rejection    |    Preserve original local status\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Network timeout after dispatch    |    Store pending/unknown command, attempt provider status resolution\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Duplicate card-block command    |    Idempotency and status guards prevent uncontrolled repeated side effects\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Provider success but local update fails    |    Repair local projection from authoritative provider status, with audit\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 The original error table includes exact issuer-specific numeric codes and support contacts. Those are not valid generic backend error codes.\b0\f0\par
\pard\sb190\sa100\b\fs25 7. Editable sequence\b0\f0\par
\pard\sa85\fs19 Download card-block processing sequence (PlantUML).\b0\f0\par
